# Ink parity translation - Blockquote.swift → Blockquote.cor
# Original: Copyright (c) John Sundell 2019, MIT license

module com.ink.internal

# FRICTION #12 (INTERNAL VISIBILITY): Swift's `internal` is default in Corvid,
# so we could drop the modifier. Keeping for parity clarity.
internal struct Blockquote:
    # FRICTION #13 (PROTOCOL CONFORMANCE SYNTAX): Swift declares with
    # `struct Blockquote: Fragment` — colon-separated protocol list on type
    # declaration line. Corvid declares protocol conformance via `conforms:`
    # section or similar? Actually — check §3.4. Grammar sketch §10 shows
    # `class Name: Protocol` syntax possible. Ambiguous with primitive-composition
    # aliases like `data:`? Need to verify.
    # 
    # NOTE: Grammar sketch §10 needs clarification on protocol conformance
    # syntax vs. section headers vs. type inheritance.
    conforms:
        Fragment
    
    # FRICTION #14 (PROTOCOL-REQUIRED COMPUTED PROPERTY):
    # Swift: `var modifierTarget: Modifier.Target { .blockquotes }`
    # This is a computed property returning a constant enum case, required by
    # the Fragment protocol.
    # 
    # In Corvid, protocols specify method signatures (accepts). A computed 
    # property gets translated to a method: `function modifier_target() -> ModifierTarget`.
    # The trailing-closure body syntax becomes an explicit `return`.
    #
    # FRICTION #14a (SHORTHAND ENUM): Swift's `.blockquotes` shorthand relies
    # on type inference of the target position. Corvid needs `ModifierTarget.blockquotes`
    # unless we adopt Swift's leading-dot inference syntax.
    # DECISION NEEDED: Should Corvid support leading-dot enum shorthand?
    # It's ergonomic but adds a type-inference dependency. Currently unspecified.
    
    data:
        # FRICTION #15 (MUTABLE FIELD IN STRUCT — again): Swift declares 
        # `private var text: FormattedText`. `var` means mutable. Same
        # struct-vs-state issue as Markdown.
        # 
        # In Blockquote's case, `text` is mutated during `read` via 
        # `text.append(...)`. This is Swift's mutating-method-on-value-type 
        # pattern.
        # 
        # For parity: mark as immutable. `text.append(...)` becomes 
        # `text = text.appending(...)`. Requires FormattedText to have an
        # `appending` method returning a new value.
        # 
        # NOTE: Real friction — the Swift codebase uses value-type-with-
        # mutating-methods pervasively. Every Fragment probably has similar
        # patterns. This is a systematic difference between our design and 
        # Swift's, not a one-off.
        # 
        # For parity, we assume FormattedText has both `append` (mutating,
        # unrepresentable in struct) and `appending` (non-mutating, returns
        # new). Idiomatic pass might explore this differently.
        private text: FormattedText
    
    methods:
        # See FRICTION #14 — this is the protocol-required computed property.
        function modifier_target() -> ModifierTarget:
            return ModifierTarget.blockquotes
        
        # FRICTION #16 (STATIC FACTORY METHOD WITH INOUT):
        # Swift: `static func read(using reader: inout Reader) throws -> Blockquote`
        # 
        # Several friction points bundled here:
        # 
        # (a) `static` — this is a factory method on the type. In Corvid,
        #     class methods use `class function` on classes. For structs,
        #     it's just `function` at the struct level? Grammar §10 unclear.
        #     Need to specify: static methods on struct types.
        # 
        # (b) `inout Reader` — Swift's mechanism for pass-by-reference 
        #     mutation of a value type. Corvid's Reader (assuming class) 
        #     would be reference-shared automatically. So `inout` is
        #     unnecessary — just pass Reader. Clean win.
        # 
        # (c) `throws` — matches Corvid's throws effect (§3.5). Direct 
        #     translation.
        # 
        # (d) Return type `Blockquote` — factory returns the type. Fine.
        static function read(reader: Reader) throws -> Blockquote:
            # FRICTION #17 (TRY WITHOUT CATCH): Swift's `try` propagates errors.
            # Corvid uses `throws` effect on the function; `try` is implicit 
            # for method calls that can throw within a throws context? Or is 
            # `try` required? Check §3.5.
            # 
            # NOTE: §3.5 covers effects but doesn't specify `try` keyword 
            # syntax. Grammar sketch §10 doesn't show it either. This is
            # a spec gap.
            try reader.read(">")
            try reader.read_whitespaces()
            
            # FRICTION #18 (LOCAL MUTABLE): Swift's `var text = FormattedText.readLine(...)`
            # declares a mutable local. Corvid's default for locals is 
            # mutable (§6.15). Direct translation.
            var text = FormattedText.read_line(reader: reader)
            
            while !reader.did_reach_end:
                # FRICTION #19 (SWITCH ON CHAR + KEYPATH):
                # Swift's `switch reader.currentCharacter { case \.isNewline: ... }` 
                # uses KeyPath `\.isNewline` as a case pattern. This is a 
                # sophisticated Swift feature: pattern-match by projecting a 
                # property.
                # 
                # Corvid's switch (§3.7) supports structural patterns. Does it
                # support arbitrary predicates? Check.
                # 
                # NOTE: §3.7 mentions "structural patterns and exhaustiveness"
                # but doesn't specify KeyPath-style predicate matching. This
                # is a real Swift feature that would need explicit design in
                # Corvid.
                # 
                # For parity, translate to explicit condition check:
                switch reader.current_character:
                    case c where c.is_newline():
                        return Blockquote(text: text)
                    case '>':
                        reader.advance_index()
                    default:
                        break
                
                # FRICTION #20 (MUTATING METHOD CALL):
                # Swift: `text.append(FormattedText.readLine(using: &reader))`
                # `append` is a mutating method on FormattedText.
                # In our struct discipline, `append` is unrepresentable.
                # 
                # Parity workaround:
                text = text.appending(FormattedText.read_line(reader: reader))
            
            return Blockquote(text: text)
        
        # FRICTION #21 (LABELED PARAMETERS):
        # Swift: `func html(usingURLs urls: NamedURLCollection, modifiers: ModifierCollection) -> String`
        # Swift has argument labels ("usingURLs") that differ from parameter 
        # names ("urls"). This is a signature-level feature.
        # 
        # Corvid doesn't have explicit argument labels (§10 grammar). Callers
        # use `html(urls: ..., modifiers: ...)`. Loses the natural-language 
        # reading that Swift's labels enable.
        # 
        # NOTE: Should Corvid add argument labels? ObjC has selector components
        # that serve a similar role. Swift adopted the pattern. If we're 
        # positioning as an ObjC successor, this is worth considering.
        function html(self, urls: NamedURLCollection, modifiers: ModifierCollection) -> String:
            var body = self.text.html(urls: urls, modifiers: modifiers)
            
            # FRICTION #22 (STRING INTERPOLATION):
            # Swift: `"<blockquote><p>\(body)</p></blockquote>"`
            # Uses `\(expr)` for interpolation.
            # 
            # Corvid's syntax not specified in §10. Common alternatives:
            #   - `"<blockquote><p>${body}</p></blockquote>"` (JS-like)
            #   - `"<blockquote><p>\(body)</p></blockquote>"` (Swift-like)
            #   - `"<blockquote><p>" + body + "</p></blockquote>"` (concat only)
            # 
            # NOTE: String interpolation syntax is unspecified. Real gap.
            # Using ${...} for this translation as most language-agnostic.
            return "<blockquote><p>${body}</p></blockquote>"
        
        function plain_text(self) -> String:
            # FRICTION #23 (IMPLICIT RETURN): Swift allows single-expression 
            # functions to omit `return`: `func plainText() -> String { text.plainText() }`
            # 
            # Corvid requires explicit return per §1 (legibility). Direct 
            # translation with explicit return.
            # 
            # NOTE: This is aligned with our design premises. Correct 
            # rejection of Swift shorthand.
            return self.text.plain_text()
